    <string name="notifications_readme_desc">About controlling and debugging notifications</string>
    <string name="notifications_readme_control">Katapult only shows notification badges, it doesn\'t manage notifications itself. Use App Notifications, an Android settings screen, to choose which apps can notify you and turn any of them off.</string>
    <string name="notifications_readme_debug">If you hear a notification but can\'t find it, open the Notification Log to see every notification the Android system has posted and track down where it came from.</string>
    <string name="screensaver_readme_desc">About the screensaver and how to open it</string>
    <string name="screensaver_readme_accessibility">Open on Power Button needs the Katapult Action Service enabled.</string>
    <string name="screensaver_readme_about">This uses the screensaver to mimic an Always On Display. It shows the clock, date, battery, notification counts and wallpaper, like the home screen.</string>
    <string name="screensaver_readme_use">Open it with a pinch-in (zoom-out) gesture on home, from the home long-press menu, or turn on Open on Power Button so it appears after you lock the phone. If Double-Tap Brightness is on, the screen stays dark and a double-tap lights it up. To exit, press Back or Home. While it\'s showing, press power to turn the display off again.</string>
    <string name="screensaver_readme_modes">Update Mode sets how often it refreshes: Static never updates (best for wallpapers), Auto updates in real time, and Timed updates every few minutes. This is not a true AOD, the phone doesn\'t go idle, so don\'t leave it on all the time, it can use more power than the display being fully off.</string>
    <string name="eink_readme_desc">About the E-Ink refresh modes</string>
    <string name="eink_readme_intro">E-Ink Mode changes how the screen refreshes. E-Ink panels hold the last image with no power and only redraw when needed, so these modes tune the trade-off between crisp text and how often the screen updates.</string>
    <string name="eink_readme_modes">Disabled is the default for third-party apps and leaves refreshing to the system. Contrast is the mode Mudita uses for its own apps. Clear and Reading refresh more aggressively for sharper text, so you may see the screen flash or update more often.</string>
    <string name="gestures_readme_desc">About page navigation and swipe gestures</string>
    <string name="gestures_readme_nav">By default you navigate pages horizontally, or with the arrow buttons. You can change the gesture direction to vertical and hide the arrows if you prefer.</string>
    <string name="gestures_readme_apps">Swipe for All Apps lets you swipe up or left to open the All Apps page, depending on the gesture direction.</string>
